# The set of pipeline stages that must run for the M4 gate to mean anything.
#
# Plan section 27 M4: "1v1 battle on same issue, isolated workspaces, public
# weights, judge run, replay URL shareable, XP/rep applied. DoD: share replay link
# with a logged-out user and they see the full timeline." Plan section 40 lists
# the replay-render assertions as M4's to hold, and section 17.2 puts the
# 100x20 ev/s load test here: "add load test simulating 100x20 ev/s before M4."
#
# Section 17.2 is why the last three stages exist and the M0 manifest has no
# equivalent. A load test in the M0 gate would run on every commit and would say
# nothing about whether the batching held; one that runs as a pre-M4 milestone
# stage says exactly that, which is the only time the question is worth asking.
#
# scripts/check-stage-manifest.sh asserts this manifest, CANONICAL_STAGES in
# test-m4.sh, and test-m4.sh's run_stage dispatch all agree — the same three-way
# check M0 uses, for the same reason.
compose
migrations
seed
# `battle-to-replay` is section 27 M4's list, end to end and in order: one issue,
# two fighters, two workspaces that cannot see each other, a rubric published
# while the battle is still running, a judge run, and a finished battle whose
# outcome reached both fighters' XP and reputation. Each of those is a claim that
# a project which shipped any four of them would still be making.
battle-to-replay
# `public-boundary` is the DoD itself — the LOGGED-OUT read. It is a separate
# stage because it is the clause that distinguishes M4 from "a battle page
# exists", and because the honest way to settle it is split: the read model is
# driven with no credential of any kind, and the files a logged-out request
# actually reaches are asserted to contain no authentication at all. The second
# half is the one that catches the regression the DoD invites, which is somebody
# adding a session check to a page that used not to have one.
public-boundary
# `load-test` is section 17.2's scenario at section 7.2's pass criterion: 100
# agents x 20 ev/s, and the number of those events allowed to become rows. Run at
# its real thresholds, with a real database-free in-process harness, because a
# load test that prints numbers and exits 0 gates nothing.
load-test
# `load-threshold` is the stage that makes the stage above believable. It runs
# the same harness twice with its thresholds deliberately set so the measurement
# cannot satisfy them, and asserts both go red. A threshold nobody has watched
# fail is a number in a file, which is the exact failure ba-risk-gates-e74 was
# opened over: "a gate nobody runs is a comment."
load-threshold
